{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you, take for yourself.</b> The plain sense of the verse is that he himself should take the anointing oil from his own. Therefore it says <b>ואתה</b> — meaning, this commandment you shall perform yourself, and it is not like the rest of the commandments that I command you in person and my intent is that others shall perform them. And although the verse included the oil and the spices in the public donations, as it is written (Exodus 25:3): ״וזאת התרומה וגו׳ בשמים לשמן המשחה״ and so forth, here HaShem revealed to him that He desires specifically Moses' own for these particular items." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained based on what is taught in Karetot (Keritot 5a) — the oil that Moses made, in it they boiled, etc.; with it the priests were anointed for seven days, etc.; and all of it endures for the future — end of quotation. From this you have learned that no anointing oil was made other than what he made. And so Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of Hilkhot Kelei HaMikdash that no other oil was ever made besides what Moses made, and that is the meaning of His saying <b>ואתה קח לך</b> — you shall make it and not another. And He hinted, in saying <b>קח לך</b>, according to their words that they said (Yalkut Shimoni 624), that Moses is destined to stand and minister in the time to come. And although in the section of the incense it is said (Exodus 30:34) ״קח לך״, there it does not say ״ואתה״." "<b>And cinnamon, fragrant, half of it.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Keritot 5a): half a hin, which means you must bring five hundred. And they raised the difficulty in the Gemara: but perhaps cinnamon fragrant is two hundred and fifty, like fragrant cane? And it answers, if so, let the verse write ״cinnamon fragrant and fragrant cane, half and half, two hundred and fifty.״ End of quotation." "And it is possible for us to answer further, that if so there would have been no need to say ״half of it,״ for I know that two hundred and fifty is half of five hundred. But if we say ״half of it״ of cinnamon, it comes to say that it is weighed out two times." "And what I saw from Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, who wrote that it is not the custom of the Ishmaelites to add even a mustard seed to the weight — this is not true, for in any case the elimination of all imbalance requires [recognition], and even with gold weights, although they are clever to weigh with the precision of one pan against its fellow without [apparent] imbalance, nevertheless when two weights are combined the imbalance becomes all the more recognizable. And the reason that the Talmud did not choose to answer as we do, is that one may say: it always states only two hundred and fifty alone, and His saying <b>half of it</b> means to bring one hundred twenty-five alone and one hundred twenty-five alone."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {1 "The Yalkut Shimoni citation reference תשס״ד (624) is rendered as given in the text.", 2 "I restored the opening <b>וקנמן בשם מחציתו</b> as lemma head, which appears to have been omitted before רז״ל in the source text.", 4 "The phrase צריך הכרת ההכרעה is rendered hyper-literally; the argument concerns the threshold at which imbalance between scale pans becomes detectable."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:53.389759937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}